course: dictionary
lesson: 13526
title: Impermeable: letting nothing through
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Impermeable is a long word, but you can break it up. The start im- means "not", as in impossible, and permeable means letting something pass through. So impermeable means not letting water, air or other liquids and gases pass through. «The tent's cover is impermeable, so not one drop of rain got inside.»
explain: Scientists use this word to test materials. Plastic, glass and rubber are impermeable, so water cannot soak through them. A sponge is not impermeable, because water passes straight into it.
explain: Impermeable can also describe a person. «Sam stayed impermeable to the teasing, and it never seemed to bother him.» Here it means he was not affected by what people said, as if the criticism could not get through to him.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in "not letting water through" and read the sentence again. If it still makes sense, you have got it. For the person meaning, swap in "not affected by" and check the same way.
fact: Impermeable means not letting water, air or other liquids and gases pass through.
fact: «Impermeable» can describe a person who is not affected by criticism.
fact: Plastic, glass and rubber are impermeable materials, so they are useful for keeping liquids in.
fact: Clay and granite are impermeable rocks. Rain runs off them instead of soaking in, which can cause floods.
fact: A raincoat must be impermeable. The opposite word is permeable, which means letting liquids or gases pass through.
exercise: «The raincoat was impermeable, so the rain could not soak through.» What does impermeable mean here? | Very cold and warm | Light and easy to carry | Full of tiny holes | *Water cannot pass through it
exercise: Clay is an impermeable rock. What does this tell us about rain falling on clay? | *Rain runs off it instead of soaking in | Rain soaks into it very quickly | Rain turns into snow on it | Clay makes the rain warmer
exercise: Which material is impermeable, so water cannot pass through it? | *Plastic | Sand | Cotton wool | Sponge
test: «Whatever Mia's brother said, she stayed impermeable and kept smiling.» What does impermeable suggest? | She was soaked through by the teasing | *She was not affected by what he said | She was easily upset and cried | She was very loud and angry
test: Why must a tent be made from impermeable cloth? | To make it see-through | To let air flow through quickly | To make it heavy and hard to carry | *To keep the rain out
exam: «The layer of impermeable clay under the field stopped the water from draining away, so the field stayed wet.» What does impermeable mean here? | Shiny and smooth to touch | Letting water pass through quickly | Very warm and dry | *Stopping water from passing through
